{% extends "docs.njk" %}

{% block doc %}

<section id="inviting">
  <h2>Inviting someone</h2>
  <ol class="docs-steps">
    <li class="docs-step">
      <h3>Open Settings → Team</h3>
      <p>From the profile menu, top right. Owners only.</p>
    </li>
    <li class="docs-step">
      <h3>Name your workspace, if you haven't</h3>
      <p>The first time you invite someone to a workspace still called "My Workspace", Distilled offers to rename it, so the invitation says <em>Acme Engineering</em> rather than something nobody recognises. You can skip it; the prompt won't come back.</p>
    </li>
    <li class="docs-step">
      <h3>Send the invite</h3>
      <p>Click <strong>Invite member</strong>, enter an email address, and send. The link arrives within about 30 seconds and is valid for 14 days.</p>
    </li>
    <li class="docs-step">
      <h3>They sign in</h3>
      <p>They click the link and sign in with GitHub. There's no approval step and no second confirmation. They land on the same dashboard you see.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="roles">
  <h2>Roles</h2>
  <div class="docs-rows">
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Owner</strong>
      <p>Full access: the dashboard plus every setting, covering team management, repositories, GitHub connections and deployment tracking. Exactly one owner per workspace.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Member</strong>
      <p>The dashboard. Every metric for every repository in the workspace, and no settings. This is the right role for most of a team.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>There's no per-repository permission model. A workspace is the visibility boundary: everyone in it sees all of its repositories.</p>
</section>

<section id="invitee">
  <h2>What it looks like for the person invited</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No Distilled account yet?</strong> The link sends them to sign in with GitHub, and they're added automatically afterwards.</li>
    <li><strong>Already have one?</strong> The link redeems against their current sign-in. The new workspace becomes their active one; the switcher gets them back.</li>
    <li><strong>Signed in without clicking the link?</strong> If the invited address is one of their verified GitHub emails, a banner appears on the dashboard offering to accept or decline.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The email on their GitHub account doesn't have to match the address you invited. The link itself is the credential, so treat it like one and send it to an address only that person can read.</p>
</section>

<section id="managing">
  <h2>Managing the team</h2>
  <p>Settings → Team lists members and pending invitations.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Remove</strong> revokes a member's access on their next interaction. Their memberships of other workspaces are unaffected.</li>
    <li><strong>Transfer ownership</strong> promotes a member to owner and makes you a regular member. Do this before you leave.</li>
    <li><strong>Resend</strong> issues a fresh link for a pending invitation and restarts the 14-day clock. The old link stops working.</li>
    <li><strong>Revoke</strong> kills a pending invitation's link.</li>
    <li><strong>Rename</strong> changes the workspace name everywhere, for everyone.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="github">
  <h2>Team membership and GitHub access</h2>
  <p>They're separate on purpose. Being a member of a Distilled workspace grants no access to your repositories. Distilled's own GitHub App holds that access, read-only, and it isn't delegated to anyone you invite. A colleague with no GitHub access to a repository can still read its delivery metrics, which is usually exactly what you want for an engineering leader or a delivery manager.</p>
</section>

